Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100 align:start position:0%
 
Oh  if you were like me spinning around in a  circle for just a few seconds is enough  to render you uncomfortably dizzy now  imagine if you had to spend every second  of every day spinning at a rate of 460  meters per second or roughly 1,000 miles  per hour well provided you're standing  near the equator you kind of already do  the earth makes one full rotation every  23 hours and 56 minutes and it's  circumference is roughly 40,000 75  kilometers which gives the surface of  our home planet a rotational speed of  416 meters per second around the equator  besides giving us what we know as our  day and night cycle the planets rotation  has profound effect on the conditions of  Earth what would happen if that rotation  suddenly stopped if we assume that some  unknown force stopped the earth  instantly the immediate effect would be  disastrous  the effect would be similar to what  you'd experience if you hit an immovable  barrier on the highway while not wearing  a seat belt except instead of traveling  at 70 miles an hour you'd be going a  thousand miles an hour the earth itself  would stop spinning but the atmosphere  liquids and most objects around the  world would be launched sideways at 460  meters per second buildings would be  leveled by winds exceeding the speed of  atomic blast shockwaves humans animals  and cars would become billions of  individual ballistic missiles the  momentum and sudden lack of gravity near  the equator would create enormous  kilometers high tsunamis that could  sweep across land masses in a matter of  minutes as the world's oceans rush  towards the poles any area is lucky  enough to survive the destructive winds  and massive tsunamis would likely be  stripped bare by more gradual erosion  from continued winds destroying animal  habitats homes and farmland the world  would suffer from massive droughts and  with most or all of the water gone or  far away there would be no way to  support life for an extended period of  time the immediate aftermath of this  planetary braking is severe and would  likely put every species on earth  including humans on the critically  endangered list if not extinct  altogether but what about the long-term  effect of an on spinning earth there  would be many less destructive effects  to the change and some serious ones that  might not be obvious at first  for example days would last much much  longer right now it takes the earth  about 24 hours to  spin on its axis marking one full day if  the planet stopped spinning gradually  say over the course of several billion  years it would eventually reach a point  where it would be considered  sun-synchronous meaning one rotation of  the earth would take 365 days similar to  tidal locking this state would cause  every spot on earth to exist in  permanent day or permanent night the  oceans would boil or evaporate entirely  on the day side of the planet and freeze  on the night side making life  exceptionally difficult if not  impossible except in the slim Twilight  regions between the day and night sides  if instead the earth reached a point  where it had stopped spinning entirely  you would experience half a year of  sunlight and half a year of darkness  which might allow for slightly more  livable conditions but would likely ruin  agriculture and plant growth cycles as  there would be no real transition  between seasons one very gradual change  would be the planets change from its  current globe shape to a more perfect  sphere right now due to the Earth's  rotation there is a slight bulge around  the equator making the earth more of a  deflated ball than a sphere because of  this bulge the area around the equator  holds more of the world's ocean water as  the earth shifted towards a more  spherical shape this water would flood  surrounding areas as it migrates towards  the poles where gravity is the strongest  this would create two enormous polar  oceans and leave one giant continent  that would wrap around the equator if  you lived in this polar ocean world you  could walk around the entire earth  without ever having to cross a body of  water  another gradual but more lethal change  would be the slow dissipation of the  Earth's magnetic field when the planet  spins molten metal from the core Rises  cools and sinks back down creating  convection currents and generating  magnetic energy if the earth stopped  spinning this process would grind to a  halt and the planet's magnetic field  would fade away leaving the surface to  be bombarded with cosmic radiation and  lethal rays from the Sun years of  constant baking from radiation would  render the earth rocky and lifeless much  like our sister planet Mars which once  had a magnetosphere of its own when Mars  lost its magnetic field the Sun slowly  stripped away the planet's atmosphere  scorching the surface and leaving the  barren red planet we know today so the  Earth's rotation is really the only  thing standing between us and planet  why disaster luckily there's not really  any chance of that spin suddenly coming  to a halt sure the planet will slowly  stop spinning but it'll be over the  course of billions of years and humanity  will most likely be extinct or long gone  by then and as far as we know there's  nothing that could stop the Earth's  rotation immediately with the exception  of maybe another planet colliding with  ours but in that case we'd be dead  either way make sure to check out my  other space related videos by clicking  this playlist or if you're really bored  you can watch all my videos by clicking  here remember to subscribe to keep up to  date with the latest content and leave a  comment down below with your thoughts on  today's topic you can also check out my  new combined Instagram with real life  lore where we share photos of our  adventures and daily life thanks for  watching and I'll see you in the next  video

